{"subjectId":"java","id":"competitive-io","title":"Competitive Programming I/O in Java","group":"Design, Modern Java & Interview Prep","order":73,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Java is fast enough for contests <em>if</em> you avoid slow input, per-line output and a few well-known traps.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-hourglass\"></i>Scanner is slow</h3>\n<p><code>Scanner</code> uses regular expressions for every token — fine for 10³ numbers, too slow for 10⁶. Read lines with <code>BufferedReader</code> and split them with <code>StringTokenizer</code>; buffer the output with <code>PrintWriter</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Speed</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>Scanner(System.in)</code></td><td>Slow (regex per token)</td><td>Small inputs, quick scripts</td></tr><tr><td><code>BufferedReader</code> + <code>StringTokenizer</code></td><td>Fast</td><td>The standard contest template</td></tr><tr><td><code>DataInputStream</code> custom byte reader</td><td>Fastest</td><td>Huge inputs (10⁷ numbers)</td></tr><tr><td><code>System.out.println</code> in a loop</td><td>Slow (flushes / locks)</td><td>Avoid for many lines</td></tr><tr><td><code>PrintWriter</code> / <code>StringBuilder</code></td><td>Fast</td><td>Build output, flush once</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-template\"></i>The standard template</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.io.*;\nimport java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {\n        BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));\n        PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(new BufferedWriter(new OutputStreamWriter(System.out)));\n\n        int t = Integer.parseInt(br.readLine().trim());          // number of test cases\n        while (t-- &gt; 0) {\n            int n = Integer.parseInt(br.readLine().trim());\n            StringTokenizer st = new StringTokenizer(br.readLine());\n            long sum = 0;                                        // long: sums overflow int\n            int max = Integer.MIN_VALUE;\n            for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) {\n                int x = Integer.parseInt(st.nextToken());\n                sum += x;\n                max = Math.max(max, x);\n            }\n            out.println(sum + \" \" + max);\n        }\n        out.flush();                                             // forget this → no output!\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>6 3\n3000000000 1000000000</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Input (stdin)</figcaption><pre><code>2\n3\n1 2 3\n3\n1000000000 1000000000 1000000000</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-bolt\"></i>A reusable FastReader</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.io.*;\nimport java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static final class FastReader {\n        private final BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));\n        private StringTokenizer st;\n\n        String next() throws IOException {\n            while (st == null || !st.hasMoreTokens()) {\n                st = new StringTokenizer(br.readLine());   // tokens may span lines\n            }\n            return st.nextToken();\n        }\n        int nextInt() throws IOException { return Integer.parseInt(next()); }\n        long nextLong() throws IOException { return Long.parseLong(next()); }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {\n        FastReader in = new FastReader();\n        int n = in.nextInt();\n        int[] a = new int[n];\n        for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) a[i] = in.nextInt();\n\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n        for (int i = n - 1; i &gt;= 0; i--) sb.append(a[i]).append(i &gt; 0 ? ' ' : '\\n');\n        System.out.print(sb);                                   // one write\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>5 4 3 2 1</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>Overflow: when to use long</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static final int MOD = 1_000_000_007;\n\n    static long power(long base, long exp, long mod) {      // fast exponentiation, O(log exp)\n        long result = 1;\n        base %= mod;\n        while (exp &gt; 0) {\n            if ((exp &amp; 1) == 1) result = result * base % mod;\n            base = base * base % mod;\n            exp &gt;&gt;= 1;\n        }\n        return result;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int a = 1_000_000;\n        System.out.println(a * a);              // int overflow, silently wraps\n        System.out.println((long) a * a);       // widen BEFORE multiplying\n        System.out.println((long) (a * a));     // too late: already overflowed\n        System.out.println(power(2, 1_000_000_000L, MOD));\n        System.out.println(Math.floorMod(-7, 3) + \" \" + (-7 % 3));   // % keeps the sign\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>-727379968\n1000000000000\n-727379968\n140625001\n2 -1</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-sort-ascending\"></i>The Arrays.sort(int[]) trap</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Hackable sort</div><p><code>Arrays.sort(int[])</code> uses dual-pivot quicksort: O(n log n) on average but <strong>O(n²) on adversarial input</strong>, and contest “anti-Java” tests exploit that. <code>Arrays.sort(Object[])</code> (e.g. <code>Integer[]</code>) uses TimSort, which is always O(n log n).</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static void safeSort(int[] a) {\n        Random rnd = new Random(42);\n        for (int i = a.length - 1; i &gt; 0; i--) {       // shuffle first → no adversarial order\n            int j = rnd.nextInt(i + 1);\n            int tmp = a[i]; a[i] = a[j]; a[j] = tmp;\n        }\n        Arrays.sort(a);\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[] a = {5, 1, 4, 2, 3};\n        safeSort(a);\n        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(a));\n\n        Integer[] boxed = {5, 1, 4, 2, 3};             // alternative: object sort = TimSort\n        Arrays.sort(boxed, Collections.reverseOrder());\n        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(boxed));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\n[5, 4, 3, 2, 1]</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-checklist\"></i>Contest checklist</h3>\n<ul><li>Class name: most judges require <code>public class Main</code> (Codeforces accepts any; check the rules).</li><li>Use <code>long</code> for sums, products and anything that can exceed ~2.1 × 10⁹.</li><li>Recursion depth &gt; ~10⁴ may overflow the default stack — iterate, or run the solver in <code>new Thread(null, task, \"\", 1 &lt;&lt; 26)</code>.</li><li><code>ArrayDeque</code> over <code>Stack</code>/<code>LinkedList</code>; <code>int[]</code> over <code>List&lt;Integer&gt;</code> in hot loops (boxing is slow).</li><li>Compare boxed values with <code>equals</code> — <code>Integer == Integer</code> fails above 127.</li><li>Flush <code>PrintWriter</code> at the end (or use <code>try-with-resources</code>).</li></ul>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why is BufferedReader faster than Scanner?</summary><div><p><code>BufferedReader</code> reads large chunks and hands back raw lines; <code>StringTokenizer</code> splits on whitespace with a simple loop. <code>Scanner</code> has a smaller buffer and parses every token with regular expressions and locale-aware number parsing, which is several times slower on large inputs.</p></div></details></section>"}